<p>Apple said Tim Cook will step down as chief executive on September 1 and hand the role to John Ternus, the company’s senior vice president of hardware engineering, ending Cook’s nearly 15-year run leading the iPhone maker. Cook, who has led Apple since 2011, will stay on as executive chairman, answering a long-running succession question around the 65-year-old executive. The transition keeps leadership in-house by elevating a longtime executive who joined Apple in 2001 and has overseen major hardware programs including the iPhone, iPad, and Mac. Several reports said the choice signals continuity at a moment when Apple is under pressure to show stronger progress in artificial intelligence and defend its position against rivals including Nvidia and Meta, even as the company remains highly profitable and recently reported record iPhone-led revenue alongside renewed demand in China.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/YxfqwTcsahY3j_Dmj2T0Bj6XzzVQB9coc753xyps4UBGs6DFucveetmTMfjrTSm9niN1wess56hz1GN_MZutvnJ5IL6u36VDVInecwVjEqWq' alt='Apple CEO Tim Cook speaks during Apple’s “Awe-Dropping” event at the Steve Jobs Theater on the Apple Park campus in Cupertino, California, on September 9, 2025.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Cook legacy: During Cook’s tenure, Apple’s market value rose from about $350 billion to around $4 trillion, and the company expanded into businesses including Apple Watch, AirPods, Apple Music, and Apple Pay.</li><li>Internal support: Creative Strategies chief executive Ben Bajarin said Ternus is well liked inside Apple and would bring “fresh energy,” while Straits Times cited an analyst saying Apple executives speak highly of him.</li><li>Product focus: At 50, Ternus takes over with a background rooted in product design and engineering rather than Cook’s operations and supply-chain expertise, marking a shift in emphasis for Apple’s top job.</li></ul><blockquote>It has been the greatest privilege of my life to be the CEO of Apple and to have been trusted to lead such an extraordinary company. - Tim Cook</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Tim Cook: Cook framed the handover as a leadership transition rather than a departure, saying he will stay on as executive chairman after September 1. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/apr/20/tim-cook-apple-ceo-replacement'>The Guardian</a>)</li><li>Semafor: Semafor saw Ternus’s elevation as a safe internal choice that suggests Apple plans to stick with its cautious, lucrative strategy during a more challenging competitive period. (<a href='https://www.semafor.com/article/04/20/2026/apple-makes-a-safe-choice-in-a-dangerous-moment'>Semafor</a>)</li><li>Ben Bajarin: Bajarin said Ternus is well regarded within Apple and argued that the new chief executive can bring fresh energy to the company. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1993605/apple-says-chief-executive-tim-cook-stepping-down-in-september'>Dawn</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.lemonde.fr/en/international/article/2026/04/21/apple-ceo-tim-cook-stepping-down-in-september-it-has-been-the-greatest-privilege-of-my-life_6752634_4.html'>Apple CEO Tim Cook stepping down in September: &#x27;It has been the greatest privilege of my life&#x27;</a> - lemonde.fr</li><li><a href='https://www.ft.com/content/5b5a466c-fc48-429d-b505-2c81d87e3615'>Apple CEO Tim Cook to hand over to John Ternus in September</a> - ft.com</li><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/tech/big-tech/article/3350792/tim-cook-step-down-apple-ceo-handing-reins-firms-hardware-leader'>Apple’s Tim Cook to step down as CEO, handing reins to John Ternus</a> - scmp.com</li><li><a href='https://www.semafor.com/article/04/20/2026/apple-makes-a-safe-choice-in-a-dangerous-moment'>Apple makes a safe choice in a dangerous moment</a> - semafor.com</li><li><a href='https://www.ndtv.com/world-news/apple-ceo-resign-fewer-new-launches-bigger-revenue-how-tim-cook-filled-steve-jobs-shoes-11386233#publisher=newsstand'>Fewer New Lau